# ARBITER — shared defaults. TRACKED IN GIT. NO CREDENTIALS, EVER.
#
# This is the configuration everybody working on this repository gets by checking it
# out. It is read last of the three files services/api/env.ts looks for:
#
#     .env           your own, ignored          wins
#     .env.share     handed to you, ignored     next
#     .env.defaults  this file, tracked         last
#
# Layered by NAME, not by file: each file sets only the names still unset, so putting
# one line in your own `.env` overrides that one line and leaves the rest of this file
# in force. You never have to copy the whole thing to change part of it.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHY THESE LIVE IN GIT AND THE KEY DOES NOT
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A model name is not a secret and IS something a team has to agree on. Two developers
# on different models get different answers from what they both believe is the same
# product, and nothing on the screen says so - that disagreement surfaces as "the
# adjudicator is inconsistent", which is a claim about the product rather than about
# the configuration. Written down here, `git log` says who changed it and when.
#
# THE API KEY IS NOT IN THIS FILE and must never be added to it. This repository is
# public: a key committed here is readable by anyone, is harvested from GitHub within
# minutes by scanners built for exactly that, and bills the Google Cloud project it is
# bound to until it is revoked. Deleting the line afterwards does not help - it stays in
# the history, and in every fork and clone taken meanwhile.
#
# To get a key, see "GETTING A KEY" at the bottom. It goes in `.env.share` or `.env`,
# both of which are gitignored.
#
# WITHOUT A KEY THE PRODUCT STILL RUNS. Cases, positions, the blind reveal, unanimity,
# the audit and the hash-chained record are pure code. Adjudication answers from a stub
# labelled `source: "stub"` so it can never be read as a model's answer; Ask and summary
# are the only surfaces that hard-require a model, and they return 503 {"error":"no_key"}.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. MODELS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# These are Gemini DEVELOPER API catalogue names, chosen to match the shared key, which
# talks to that host. Measured against it on 2026-08-16, 10 schema-constrained calls each:
#
#   gemini-3.1-flash-lite   10/10 ok
#   gemini-flash-latest      8/10 ok   (two 503 "high demand")
#   gemini-3.5-flash         2/6  ok   (503 "high demand")
#   gemini-2.5-flash-lite    404       "no longer available to new users"
#   gemini-3.5-flash-lite    400       rejects thinkingBudget 0, which the short shapes need
#
# The 503s are Google capacity on the flash tier, not this service. They surface as
# `upstream: This model is currently experiencing high demand`. Nothing retries them.

# Fallback for every call kind, and the model the short classification calls use. Those
# run under a 2.5s client abort, so the reliable one belongs here.
ARBITER_MODEL=gemini-3.1-flash-lite

# Adjudication, ask and summary all resolve through this one (interpret.ts). The better
# model, accepting the occasional 503, because adjudication is the product's actual claim.
ARBITER_ADJUDICATION_MODEL=gemini-flash-latest

# Which Google service a key talks to. The two are NOT interchangeable - they serve
# different model catalogues - so this is typed, never inferred.
#
#   developer  generativelanguage.googleapis.com. Needs no project setup, which is why
#              the shared key uses it and why the names above are its catalogue.
#   vertex     aiplatform.googleapis.com, express mode. The catalogue this project's
#              committed numbers were measured on. Needs aiplatform.googleapis.com
#              enabled on the key's project or every call is a 403 naming that project.
#
# Read only when a key is present; the ADC path always calls Vertex (gemini.ts).
ARBITER_GEMINI_HOST=developer

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 2. THE DEMONSTRATION TEAM AND THE FOUR CASES
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Creates the five demo accounts and opens the four usable library cases on first boot,
# so a fresh clone comes up showing the same product everyone else sees instead of an
# empty sign-in screen. Equivalent to `npm run seed:demo` plus opening each case from
# the library picker by hand.
#
# THE CASE CONTENT IS ALREADY IN GIT - data/cases/*.json, data/out/tak994.json and
# data/probe-case.json. This flag only decides whether they are opened for you on boot.
#
# TWO GUARDS. Accounts are seeded only into an EMPTY account store and cases only into
# an EMPTY case store, so neither can appear beside real data and neither resurrects
# something deliberately deleted. The five accounts share one published password
# (services/api/seed-demo.ts) - the authentication path is the real one, what is fake is
# the secrecy of the password.
#
# DELETE THIS LINE before this deployment holds anything that matters.
ARBITER_DEMO_SEED=1

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 3. WHAT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* SET HERE
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ARBITER_MODEL_BUDGET  Left at the code default of 30 model calls per account per 10
#                       minutes (services/api/spend.ts). That cap is what makes the paid
#                       endpoints safe to expose at all, and everybody sharing one key is
#                       spending from one budget - so a raised ceiling is a decision for
#                       whoever owns the billing, taken in their own file, not a default
#                       shipped to everyone who clones.
#
# ARBITER_HOST          Left at loopback. This process terminates no TLS.
#
# VITE_*                Anything with this prefix is compiled into the browser bundle and
#                       is readable by anyone who loads the page. Never put a credential
#                       behind a VITE_ name to "make it available to the frontend".

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 4. GETTING A KEY
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Ask the repository owner for the shared key. It arrives as one line to paste into a new
# `.env.share` file in the repository root:
#
#     GEMINI_API_KEY=AQ....
#
# That is the whole setup - no rename, no gcloud, no Google account, no project of your
# own. `.env.share` is gitignored; do not commit it and do not paste it anywhere public.
#
# Or use your own credentials instead, in your own `.env`:
#
#     GEMINI_API_KEY=...                 your own key, same shape as above
#     ARBITER_GEMINI_HOST=vertex         if the key is a Vertex one
#     ARBITER_MODEL=                     blank reverts to the repo default for that name
#     ARBITER_ADJUDICATION_MODEL=
#
# Or Application Default Credentials, which need no key at all but authenticate a PERSON
# and so cannot be shared:
#
#     gcloud auth application-default login
#     gcloud services enable aiplatform.googleapis.com
#     ARBITER_GCP_PROJECT=your-gcp-project-id
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 5. WHAT "IT WORKS" LOOKS LIKE
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#     npm ci && npm run dev          # http://localhost:5173
#
# The banner names the files it read and the models it resolved. With this file alone
# and no key, `Adjudication:` reads STUB and that is the correct, working, no-credential
# state. With a key it names the live model. If `Config:` does not list `.env.defaults`,
# you are running from somewhere other than the repository root.
